course: religion-ethics
lesson: 62
title: The church funeral
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A Christian funeral gives thanks to God for a person's life, comforts the people who are sad and commits the person to God's care; the body lies in the coffin at the front, and the person cannot feel or see anything any more.
explain: Picture Grandad's funeral at Ellie's church: dark clothes, flowers on the coffin, the vicar walking in and saying, «I am the resurrection and the life, says the Lord»; then a poem from cousin Jack, a funny story about Grandad falling in a pond that makes everyone laugh, hymns, prayers and Psalm 23.
explain: The vicar speaks in very formal, old words, such as «to remember before God our brother Peter, to give thanks for his life and commend him to God», because a funeral is solemn, and some of these words have been said for hundreds of years.
explain: People show sadness in different ways: some cry, some cry later, some laugh at the funny stories, and a four-year-old may ask loudly about McDonald's; all of it can belong at a funeral.
explain: Everyone is welcome: a Muslim friend like Mohammed can stand or sit quietly during the prayers without saying prayers he doesn't believe, and being there with respect is enough; children may choose whether to go, once a grown-up has explained what will happen.
fact: At a Christian funeral the person's body is in the coffin; the body has stopped working and cannot feel anything.
fact: Christians believe the person, their soul, is with God, and they hope for resurrection.
fact: The resurrection is the Christian belief that, because Jesus rose from the dead at Easter, those who trust God will be raised to new life with him.
fact: «I am the resurrection and the life» are words of Jesus from John's Gospel, often said at the start of a funeral.
fact: The Church of England uses funeral words from the Book of Common Prayer and Common Worship; to «commend» someone to God means to entrust them to God's care.
fact: Funerals often include poems, readings and funny stories about the person's life; laughing together can be a way of loving them.
fact: Hymns are songs to God; at funerals they give comfort and hope; «The Lord's my shepherd», based on Psalm 23, is often sung.
fact: Psalm 23 says, «Though I walk through the valley of the shadow of death, I will fear no evil, for you are with me»; «the valley of the shadow of death» is a poetic picture of the darkest times, when God is still with us; Jews and Christians both read it.
fact: Many people wear dark clothes to a funeral; it is a custom that shows respect and sadness.
fact: Pallbearers carry the coffin; they may be family, friends or people who work for the funeral director.
fact: Children can choose whether to go to a funeral, after a grown-up explains what will happen.
fact: Guests of other faiths or none can stand or sit quietly during prayers to show respect.
fact: People grieve differently; not crying does not mean not caring.
exercise: Ellie asks, «Is Grandad in the box?» What is the honest answer? | Yes, his body is in the coffin; it cannot feel or see anything | No, the coffin is empty and is only there to look at, for show | Yes, and he can still hear everything we say about him today | No, he is waiting in the car outside until the service is over
exercise: The vicar says, «I am the resurrection and the life.» What is the resurrection? | The belief that because Jesus rose, those who trust God will rise to new life | A special flower placed on coffins | The moment the coffin is carried out | A Latin name for the funeral cars
exercise: People laugh at a story about Grandad falling in a pond. Is that bad? | No, remembering funny moments can be a way of loving him | Yes, laughing at a funeral is forbidden and very rude to the family | Yes, the vicar will stop the service | No, because Grandad can hear the joke and is laughing along
exercise: The vicar says, «We have come here today to remember before God our brother Peter.» Why so posh? | Funerals are solemn, and many of the words are very old | The vicar is showing off all his long words to the people there | Posh words make the service faster | Vicars are not allowed to speak in ordinary English in church
exercise: Mia sees others crying but her mum isn't. Is her mum not sad? | People show sadness differently; some cry later or in private | Her mum didn't really love Grandad as much as the others did | Crying is not allowed at all for the closest family at a funeral | Her mum is only pretending to be strong so the vicar sees
exercise: Jacob asks why they sing hymns when everyone is sad. Why? | Singing gives comfort and hope and lets people share their feelings | Because funerals are really parties, and parties always have songs | To cover up the sound of people crying in the church benches | Because singing is the only way the vicar is allowed to end the service
exercise: What does «the valley of the shadow of death» in Psalm 23 mean? | A poetic picture of the darkest times, where God is still with us | A real valley in England full of ghosts | A place where people go when they die | The path the coffin takes out of church
exercise: Six people in black carry the coffin out. Who are they? | Pallbearers: family, friends or people from the funeral director | Grandad's football team | Police officers guarding the coffin | Priests from six different churches
exercise: Harry asks if he has to go to Grandma's funeral. What is wise? | Explain what happens and let him choose | Make him go, whatever he feels about it | Never let children go to funerals at all | Tell him nothing and just leave him at home
exercise: A four-year-old says loudly, «Can we go to McDonald's after?» How do people usually react? | Many smile; little children don't understand funerals like adults | Everyone is furious with him and he is asked to leave the church | The vicar stops the whole service until the boy is quiet again and sorry | His family are told never to come back to that church again
exercise: Mohammed, who is Muslim, is at the funeral. Does he have to say the Christian prayers? | No, he can stand or sit quietly to show respect | Yes, everyone must say every prayer | Yes, or he must wait outside until the prayers are finished | No, he must get up and leave before any of the prayers start
exercise: What does it mean to «commend» someone to God? | To entrust them to God's care | To tell God everything they did wrong | To ask God to bring them back | To give them a medal from God
exercise: Which book of the Church of England contains many old funeral words? | The Book of Common Prayer | The Book of Kells, from Ireland | The Highway Code, from the government | The Book of Good Manners, for children
exercise: Which hymn, based on Psalm 23, is often sung at funerals? | «The Lord's my shepherd» | «Twinkle, twinkle, little star» | «Happy Birthday to You» | «Jingle Bells»
exercise: Why do people wear dark clothes to a funeral? | It is a custom to show respect and sadness | Black clothes keep bad luck away from the family | The vicar insists on black for everyone who comes | Dark clothes are cheaper to buy and easier to wash
test: Ellie's little cousin whispers, «Is Grandad cold in the box?» What is gentle and true? | His body can't feel cold or anything any more | Yes, so we should give him a warm blanket in there | Yes, and he really wants to come out of the box now | No, because the box has a little heater inside it
test: The vicar intones: «We have come here today to remember before God our brother Peter, to give thanks for his life, and to commend him to God.» What three things is the service doing? | Remembering Peter, thanking God for his life, entrusting him to God | Judging Peter, punishing him, and burying him | Selling Peter's house, sharing his money, closing his bank | Waking Peter up, thanking him, and sending him home
test: A Gen Z teen says, «The vicar's lowkey giving Shakespeare, why's he talk so posh?» («Lowkey» means «a bit»; «giving» means «sounding like».) Best answer? | Formal old words show respect at a solemn time | He is showing off because he's famous | Vicars are required by the church to sound like Shakespeare | Nobody knows what he is saying, not even the vicar himself
test: Cousin Jack from Manchester reads his poem: «Me grandad were sound, he fell in t'pond once, proper soaked!» («Sound» means «a good person».) Everyone laughs. Is that okay? | Yes, funny memories are part of loving him | No, Jack should be sent out of the church for that | No, the vicar will be offended and stop the poem | Yes, but only if Grandad would have laughed too
test: What would happen if a funeral had no hymns, poems or stories, only silence? | It could still be respectful, but many people find songs and stories comforting | It would not count as a funeral at all | The person could not be buried | Everybody would stop being sad
test: A Gen Alpha boy whispers, «The valley of death is so sus, is there a monster?» («Sus» means «scary, dodgy».) What does the psalm mean? | It is a poem about the darkest times, saying God is still with us | It is a real valley somewhere in England, with a real monster living in it | It is the place where dead people go to wait for a week | It is a warning in the Bible to stay away from all valleys
test: Harry's nan in Cardiff says, «You don't have to go, bach, but it's lush to say goodbye.» («Bach» means «dear»; «lush» means «lovely».) What is she saying? | It is his choice, but saying goodbye can be good | He must go, or else he will be in big trouble at home | Funerals are like parties with lush food and cakes after | Children are never allowed at funerals in Wales, only grown-ups
test: The four-year-old shouts about McDonald's and Sophie says, «OMG he's so embarrassing.» What is a kind answer? | Nobody is cross; small children say things like that, and people smiled | He has ruined the whole funeral for everyone, and they'll all remember it | He must go up and say sorry to the coffin in front of everyone | He should be taken home at once and not allowed back in
test: Kai worries, «Does Mo have to pray our prayers?» What is the respectful truth? | No, he can stand or sit quietly; being there is enough | Yes, he must say every single word, the same as everyone else | No, so he should go and wait outside in the car park | Yes, because it's a Christian building and those are the rules
test: A cartoon shows a grandad sitting up in his coffin and waving at his funeral. What is the truth? | The person has died and cannot sit up or wave | Dead people can sit up and wave if the music is loud enough | It happens at every funeral, but grown-ups never mention it | Only grandads can do that, because they are so old and wise
test: Mia's big brother says, «Mum's not crying, she's got no heart.» What is fair? | People grieve differently; Mum may cry later or in private | He's right; she didn't really love Grandad as much as they did | Only people who cry in front of everyone are truly sad | Mum is not allowed to cry at the funeral, because of the rules
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the purposes of a Christian funeral service? | The giving of thanks for a life, the comfort of the bereaved and the commendation of the deceased to God | The determination of the inheritance of the deceased's estate | The public recital of the faults of the deceased | The restoration of the deceased to life
exam: Identify the doctrine to which the words «I am the resurrection and the life» refer. | The Christian hope of being raised to new life through Christ | The reincarnation of the soul in another body | The preservation of the body by embalming | The annual return of the dead upon All Souls' Day
exam: Select the response consistent with the established conventions concerning the attendance of persons of other faiths at a Christian funeral. | They may attend and remain respectfully silent during prayers | They are required to recite all of the Christian prayers aloud | They are excluded from attendance at the service by church law | They are required to depart before the prayers commence
exam: Ascertain the function of the persons designated «pallbearers». | The carrying of the coffin | The conducting of the hymns | The reading of the will | The digging of the grave
